INSERT INTO viewpoint_wiki (viewpoint_id, wikipedia_title, wikipedia_lang, extract_text, sections, citations, last_updated, created_at) VALUES (69752, 'Ninja', 'en', 'A ninja , shinobi no mono  or shinobi  was a spy and infiltrator in pre-modern Japan. Antecedents may have existed as early as the 12th century. There is little evidence that they were assassins.', '[{"index": "1", "level": 1, "title": "Etymology", "content": ""}, {"index": "2", "level": 1, "title": "History", "content": "History[edit] Despite many popular folktales, historical accounts of the ninja are scarce. The social origin of the ninja is seen as the reason they agree to operate in secret, trading their service for money without honor and glory.&#91;1&#93; The first dubbed ninja was a man named Otomono Sahito, though historical evidence of his existence is scarce. However, some ninjutsu books described specifically what tactics ninja should use to fight, and the scenarios in which a ninja might find themselves can be deduced from those tactics. For example, in the manuscript of volume 2 of Kanrin Seiyō (間林清陽) which is the original book of Bansenshūkai (万川集海), there are 48 points of ninja''s fighting techniques, such as how to make makibishi from bamboo, how to make footwear that makes no sound, fighting techniques when surrounded by many enemies, precautions when using swords at night, how to listen to small sounds, kuji-kiri that prevents guard dogs from barking, and so on.&#91;2&#93;&#91;3&#93; Predecessors[edit] Yamato Takeru dressed as a maidservant, preparing to kill the Kumaso leaders. Woodblock print on paper. Yoshitoshi, 1886. The title ninja has sometimes been attributed retrospectively to the semi-legendary 2nd-century prince Yamato Takeru.&#91;4&#93; Prince Shotoku, who lived from 574-622 AD, was believed to have employed a ninja spy named Otomono Sahito.&#91;5&#93; In the Kojiki, the young Yamato Takeru disguised himself as a charming maiden and assassinated two chiefs of the Kumaso people.&#91;6&#93; However, these records take place at a very early stage of Japanese history, and they are unlikely to be connected to the shinobi of later accounts. The first recorded use of espionage was under the employment of Prince Shōtoku in the 6th century.&#91;7&#93; Such tactics were considered unsavory even in early times, when, according to the 10th-century Shōmonki, the boy spy Hasetsukabe no Koharumaru was killed for spying against the insurgent Taira no Masakado.&#91;8&#93; The history of Ninjitsu school recorded around 12th century, when Daisuke Togakure and Kain Doshi formalized the teaching of Chinese and native Japanese guerilla tactics to which somewhat considered as counter-culture of the time. As a samurai, Daisuke lost his lands and title after defeated in a regional conflict. Refused to committed Seppuku, Daisuke travelled to the mountains of southwest Honshu in 1162. Here, he met Doshi, a Chinese warrior-monk. Daisuke abandon his way of bushido, and worked with Doshi formulate the guerilla art of war called ninjutsu. Daisuke''s descendants founded the first ninja-ryu, org ninja school, the Togakureryu.&#91;5&#93; Later, the 14th-century war chronicle Taiheiki contained many references to shinobi&#91;4&#93; and credited the destruction of a castle by fire to an unnamed but \"highly skilled shinobi\". Early history[edit] It was not until the 15th century that spies were specially trained for their purpose. It was around this time that the word shinobi appeared to define and clearly identify ninja as a secretive group of agents. Evidence for this can be seen in historical documents, which began to refer to stealthy soldiers as shinobi during the Sengoku period. Later manuals regarding espionage are often grounded in Chinese military strategy, quoting works such as The Art of War by Sun Tzu.&#91;9&#93; By the Sengoku period, the shinobi had several roles, including spy (kanchō), scout (teisatsu), surprise attacker (kishu), and agitator (konran). The ninja families were organized into larger guilds, each with their own territories.&#91;10&#93; A system of rank existed. A jōnin (\"upper person\") was the highest rank, representing the group and hiring out mercenaries. This is followed by the chūnin (\"middle person\"), assistants to the jōnin. At the bottom was the genin (\"lower person\"), field agents drawn from the lower class and assigned to carry out actual missions.&#91;11&#93; Iga and Kōga clans[edit] .mw-parser-output .hatnote{font-style:italic}.mw-parser-output div.hatnote{padding-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.5em}.mw-parser-output .hatnote i{font-style:normal}.mw-parser-output .hatnote+link+.hatnote{margin-top:-0.5em}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .hatnote{display:none!important}}Main articles: Iga ikki, Kōka ikki, and Iga–Kōka alliance The plains of Iga, nested in secluded mountains, gave rise to villages specialized in the training of ninja. The Iga and Kōga \"clans\" were jizamurai families living in the province of Iga (modern Mie Prefecture) and the adjacent region of Kōka (later written as Kōga), named after a village in what is now Shiga Prefecture. From these regions, villages devoted to the training of ninja first appeared.&#91;12&#93; The remoteness and inaccessibility of the surrounding mountains in Iga may have had a role in the ninja''s secretive development.&#91;11&#93; The chronicle Go Kagami Furoku writes, of the two clans'' origins: .mw-parser-output .templatequote{overflow:hidden;margin:1"}, {"index": "3", "level": 2, "title": "Predecessors", "content": ""}, {"index": "4", "level": 2, "title": "Early history", "content": "Early history[edit] It was not until the 15th century that spies were specially trained for their purpose. It was around this time that the word shinobi appeared to define and clearly identify ninja as a secretive group of agents. Evidence for this can be seen in historical documents, which began to refer to stealthy soldiers as shinobi during the Sengoku period. Later manuals regarding espionage are often grounded in Chinese military strategy, quoting works such as The Art of War by Sun Tzu.&#91;1&#93; By the Sengoku period, the shinobi had several roles, including spy (kanchō), scout (teisatsu), surprise attacker (kishu), and agitator (konran). The ninja families were organized into larger guilds, each with their own territories.&#91;2&#93; A system of rank existed. A jōnin (\"upper person\") was the highest rank, representing the group and hiring out mercenaries. This is followed by the chūnin (\"middle person\"), assistants to the jōnin. At the bottom was the genin (\"lower person\"), field agents drawn from the lower class and assigned to carry out actual missions.&#91;3&#93; ^ Ratti &amp; Westbrook 1991, p.&#160;324 ^ Ratti &amp; Westbrook 1991, p.&#160;327 ^ Draeger &amp; Smith 1981, p.&#160;121"}, {"index": "5", "level": 2, "title": "Iga and Kōga clans", "content": ""}, {"index": "6", "level": 2, "title": "Iga Ninja theory''s controversy", "content": ""}, {"index": "7", "level": 2, "title": "Activities under Tokugawa", "content": ""}, {"index": "8", "level": 2, "title": "Shimabara rebellion", "content": ""}, {"index": "9", "level": 2, "title": "Edo period", "content": ""}, {"index": "10", "level": 3, "title": "Ninja stereotypes in theatre", "content": ""}, {"index": "11", "level": 2, "title": "Contemporary", "content": ""}, {"index": "12", "level": 1, "title": "Roles", "content": ""}, {"index": "13", "level": 2, "title": "Sabotage", "content": ""}, {"index": "14", "level": 2, "title": "Countermeasures", "content": ""}, {"index": "15", "level": 1, "title": "Famous people", "content": ""}, {"index": "16", "level": 1, "title": "In popular culture", "content": ""}, {"index": "17", "level": 1, "title": "Gallery", "content": ""}, {"index": "18", "level": 1, "title": "See also", "content": ""}, {"index": "19", "level": 1, "title": "Notes", "content": ""}, {"index": "20", "level": 1, "title": "References", "content": ""}, {"index": "21", "level": 2, "title": "Citations", "content": ""}, {"index": "22", "level": 2, "title": "Sources", "content": ""}, {"index": "23", "level": 1, "title": "Further reading", "content": ""}, {"index": "24", "level": 1, "title": "External links", "content": ""}]'::jsonb, '[{"ref": "wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja", "text": "Wikipedia"}]'::jsonb, '2026-01-13 14:40:21.128947+11:00', '2026-01-13 00:25:13.160767+11:00');
